d3d9: one shader variable for a sampler's binding

DXVK declares, for every sampler of a Direct3D 9 shader, a colour variable and a
depth-comparing one at the same binding, and picks between them when sampling by a constant
set per draw; for shaders older than 2.0 it declares one of each kind of texture (2D, cube,
3D), all at that binding. Metal has no two resources at one index. MoltenVK's converter turns
such a shader into Metal code that does not compile ("use of undeclared identifier
's0_2d_shadowSmplr'" with argument buffers, "cannot reserve 'texture' resource location at
index 0" without), the pipeline is not made, and nothing that samples a texture is drawn:
Portal 2 was a black screen with sound.

src/dxso/dxso_options.h, dxso_options.cpp, src/d3d9/d3d9_options.h, d3d9_options.cpp
- A new option, d3d9.singleSamplerVariant, on unless set to False.

src/dxso/dxso_compiler.cpp
- With it, a sampler gets the colour variable only, and a shader older than 2.0 a 2D one.

src/d3d9/d3d9_device.cpp
- With it, the sampler of a depth texture does not compare either.

What is lost: a depth texture is sampled as plain values, so shadows a game draws by sampling
a depth texture with comparison are not right, and a shader older than 2.0 cannot sample a
cube or 3D texture.

tests/runtime/d3d9-draw.c draws with a pixel shader that samples a texture.

